# LinkSys Runtime Status

This file summarizes the runtime boundary. The canonical resource contract is
the [typed `SystemConfig` definition](../../../edgion-resources/src/resources/link_sys/mod.rs)
and the [current LinkSys feature schema](../../../skills/02-features/03-resources/18-link-sys.md).

## Ownership boundary

`edgion-common/src/link_sys_core/` owns backend-independent credential, TLS,
health, HTTP-client, and lifecycle primitives. It also owns the Etcd provider
because the Controller and Gateway both need it. `edgion-gateway/src/link_sys/`
owns the data-plane runtime store, Gateway-only providers, and logging sinks.

`LinkSysStore` keeps the authoritative resource snapshot and dispatches full and
partial configuration updates to the provider-specific runtime state.

| `SystemConfig` variant | Runtime owner | Runtime access or consumer path |
|---|---|---|
| `Redis` | Gateway `providers/redis` plus the typed runtime store | `get_redis_client("namespace/name")` |
| `Etcd` | Shared `edgion-common/src/link_sys_core/providers/etcd` client, published by the Gateway runtime store | `get_etcd_client("namespace/name")` |
| `Elasticsearch` | Gateway `providers/elasticsearch` plus the typed runtime store | `get_es_client("namespace/name")` |
| `Kafka` | Gateway `providers/kafka` plus the typed runtime store | `get_kafka_client("namespace/name")` |
| `Otlp` | Gateway `providers/otlp` shared exporter, batch processor and probe | `get_otlp_client("namespace/name")` |
| `Webhook` | Gateway `providers/webhook` with its specialized manager and runtime store | `get_webhook_call_handle` and `resolve_webhook_key` |
| `HttpDns` | Shared `edgion-common/src/dns` resolver core | Gateway backend resolution reads the config from `LinkSysStore`; there is no generic typed LinkSys client |
| `CredentialSource` | Controller credential manager | No Gateway LinkSys client; resolved credentials arrive through the consuming plugin runtime |

### Webhook publication

Webhook does not use the generic replace-and-shutdown lifecycle. `LinkSysStore`
reserves a monotonically increasing dispatch sequence synchronously with source
snapshot publication. `WebhookManager` rejects stale sequences and holds one
mutation guard across preparation and commit. A full-set commit replaces the
manager map atomically and retires displaced registrations before the matching
Webhook runtime map is published. This ordered, specialized path prevents a
delayed full or partial dispatch from restoring older endpoints, credentials,
templates, or clients after newer configuration has been accepted.

## Log outputs

Log output configuration is `StringOutput`, not a `SystemConfig` variant.

| Output | LinkSys-backed | Runtime behavior |
|---|---|---|
| `LocalFile` | No | Gateway-owned background writer with bounded queue, rotation, retention, and disk-health checks |
| Elasticsearch (`Es`) | Yes | References an Elasticsearch LinkSys resource; `EsLogSender` resolves the current typed client |
| `Kafka` | Yes | References a Kafka LinkSys resource and topic; `KafkaLogSender` resolves the current typed client |
| `Otlp` | Yes | References one OTLP/gRPC LinkSys resource; all async log types share its current pipeline |

Elasticsearch, Kafka and OTLP log outputs use LinkSys runtime clients.
LocalFile owns its writer directly. OTLP configuration dispatch preserves source
event order before async provider dispatch. Failed candidate preparation retains
the active generation; removal and process shutdown join its owned cleanup.

### Delivery and degradation

An async logger has one primary output and, optionally, one fallback. Senders
are checked in registration order and each entry is sent to the first sender
whose live `healthy()` signal is true.

- An error from the selected sender does not fall through to the next sender.
  The entry is dropped and `edgion_access_log_dropped_total` is incremented.
- If every configured sender is unhealthy, the entry is dropped and the same
  metric is incremented.
- A zero-sender logger represents logging disabled by the operator and does not
  increment the dropped metric.

For access logs, an Elasticsearch primary with a bounded LocalFile fallback
additionally supports operator-triggered, at-least-once backfill from LocalFile
to Elasticsearch through `POST /api/v1/access-log/backfill`. This manual replay
is access-log-only and is not automatic durable replay.

There is currently no `failedCache` LinkSys field, Redis spill target, generic
spill-on-send-error mechanism, or automatic replay after a provider recovers.
Configuration that assumes any of those behaviors is invalid.
